WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.953 --> 00:00:04.443
Excited about our conversation today

00:00:04.443 --> 00:00:07.095
with Khan Academy's head of
engineering VP of engineering,

00:00:07.095 --> 00:00:09.623
Marta Kosarchyn,

00:00:09.623 --> 00:00:10.753
but before we get into that,

00:00:10.753 --> 00:00:14.406
I will give my standard
announcement a reminder to everyone

00:00:14.406 --> 00:00:16.633
that Khan Academy is a not for profit

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:19.253
that we only exist because
of philanthropic donations

00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:20.623
from folks like yourself.

00:00:20.623 --> 00:00:22.913
A special thanks to several
corporations that stepped up

00:00:22.913 --> 00:00:24.663
a couple of months ago,
when they found out

00:00:24.663 --> 00:00:26.913
that we were running at a
deficit even before COVID.

00:00:26.913 --> 00:00:29.343
And then with all of the
things we've been trying to do

00:00:29.343 --> 00:00:32.393
in response to COVID, our
deficit has only increased.

00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:34.146
Special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:00:34.146 --> 00:00:37.365
AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis
and many other foundations

00:00:37.365 --> 00:00:41.513
and individual donors of all
levels who make this possible.

00:00:41.513 --> 00:00:43.393
I will say that we still
are running at a deficit

00:00:43.393 --> 00:00:44.403
and digging into reserves.

00:00:44.403 --> 00:00:47.833
So if at any scale, if
you're in a position to help,

00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:50.843
please think about making a donation.

00:00:50.843 --> 00:00:55.163
So with that, I am
excited to introduce Marta

00:00:59.161 --> 00:01:00.711
- So, Marta, thanks for joining.

00:01:00.711 --> 00:01:02.491
And I'll remind everyone who's watching

00:01:02.491 --> 00:01:04.961
on Facebook or YouTube,
please put questions you have

00:01:04.961 --> 00:01:08.041
for myself or Marta, on
the message boards below.

00:01:08.041 --> 00:01:10.431
We have team members, who are
gonna be surfacing questions,

00:01:10.431 --> 00:01:12.120
and they can really be a questions about

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.611
anything and everything.

00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:16.351
But we're gonna be talking a
lot about just Marta's journey

00:01:16.351 --> 00:01:18.091
to how she got to Khan Academy.

00:01:18.091 --> 00:01:19.561
So it could be about career advice,

00:01:19.561 --> 00:01:20.741
especially if you're trying to think about

00:01:20.741 --> 00:01:22.841
going into technical field or engineering,

00:01:22.841 --> 00:01:24.121
but it literally could be about anything.

00:01:24.121 --> 00:01:27.050
It could be life advice, it
could be how we do engineering,

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.971
really any topic is within bounds.

00:01:29.971 --> 00:01:33.141
So Marta, maybe a good place to start.

00:01:33.141 --> 00:01:34.629
You're the CTO and VP of engineering.

00:01:34.629 --> 00:01:37.521
What do those titles mean?

00:01:37.521 --> 00:01:39.024
What do you do here at Khan Academy?

00:01:39.024 --> 00:01:41.524
What do they mean generally
in most organizations?

00:01:42.721 --> 00:01:45.882
- Yeah, I mean, generally
in most organizations,

00:01:45.882 --> 00:01:50.621
and at Khan Academy, it's
leading the engineering team,

00:01:50.621 --> 00:01:52.961
which is the team that builds our product,

00:01:52.961 --> 00:01:54.951
our Khan Academy website,

00:01:54.951 --> 00:01:57.970
and provides for learning experiences

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:02.071
that encapsulate the content
that we have available

00:02:02.071 --> 00:02:06.311
for our users and helps them
on their learning journey

00:02:06.311 --> 00:02:09.381
and all of the things
that you often talk about

00:02:09.381 --> 00:02:10.641
on daily homeroom.

00:02:10.641 --> 00:02:11.474
So we build that.

00:02:11.474 --> 00:02:14.371
And so we have to choose the
technology that's appropriate

00:02:14.371 --> 00:02:18.471
for us to not only build an
appropriate learning experience,

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.981
but build it in a way that
provides reasonable performance

00:02:21.981 --> 00:02:24.370
and stability and reliability.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.671
Because that's our
promise, that's our service

00:02:27.671 --> 00:02:30.361
to the organization is that we will

00:02:32.872 --> 00:02:34.831
we will take care of the platform,

00:02:34.831 --> 00:02:37.491
we basically steward the
platform and make the choices

00:02:37.491 --> 00:02:38.324
that will

00:02:39.651 --> 00:02:40.954
get our product out there.

00:02:41.981 --> 00:02:43.151
- Yeah, and you know, explain to,

00:02:43.151 --> 00:02:45.751
I think a lot of people have
a perception that you know,

00:02:45.751 --> 00:02:46.961
it is true Khan Academy

00:02:46.961 --> 00:02:49.081
was started by me in a walk in closet

00:02:49.081 --> 00:02:50.821
actually was this walk
in closet that I'm in now

00:02:50.821 --> 00:02:53.169
so it's still that part
is still has held true.

00:02:53.169 --> 00:02:55.561
There's a silent walk in closet someplace.

00:02:55.561 --> 00:02:57.241
It's very nice one, as you can tell,

00:02:57.241 --> 00:02:59.311
it has a bookshelf and
has some natural lighting.

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:02.271
But you know, people don't realize

00:03:02.271 --> 00:03:04.571
that we're over 200 person organization,

00:03:04.571 --> 00:03:06.760
we have an over 80
person engineering team.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.901
And so people think, Well, you know,

00:03:08.901 --> 00:03:12.864
I know Khan Academy has videos,
you have some exercises.

00:03:14.631 --> 00:03:16.854
This is a major organization.

00:03:19.327 --> 00:03:22.691
What is requiring us to have
such a, not only a large team,

00:03:22.691 --> 00:03:24.871
but a deep team, we have
some of the world's experts

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:27.923
in a lot of the areas of
technology and infrastructure.

00:03:27.923 --> 00:03:31.304
What is the focus of that
engineering team that you lead?

00:03:32.871 --> 00:03:35.471
- Yeah, that's a great
question for us to talk about

00:03:35.471 --> 00:03:37.493
because I'll share a story

00:03:37.493 --> 00:03:42.224
when I shared with folks that
I was coming to Khan Academy,

00:03:42.224 --> 00:03:46.111
and they said, Well,
what kind of engineering

00:03:46.111 --> 00:03:47.421
do you need a Khan Academy?

00:03:47.421 --> 00:03:49.661
Because so many people think, Oh, well,

00:03:49.661 --> 00:03:51.924
there are some videos right on YouTube,

00:03:51.924 --> 00:03:53.631
that you can go watch.

00:03:53.631 --> 00:03:57.121
And so I hope I was helpful
in kind of getting the word

00:03:57.121 --> 00:04:01.705
out there to come to our site
and our product and to enjoy.

00:04:01.705 --> 00:04:04.461
Not only the videos, but the articles

00:04:04.461 --> 00:04:07.871
and the exercises in particular,
and the mastery mechanics

00:04:07.871 --> 00:04:10.591
and all of the things that
we provide for learners

00:04:10.591 --> 00:04:12.796
and for teachers and for parents.

00:04:12.796 --> 00:04:16.881
So we have yes, over 80 engineers,

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:20.611
and we have a really, really
spectacular engineering team.

00:04:20.611 --> 00:04:22.831
We're so fortunate to have

00:04:24.585 --> 00:04:26.971
incredibly skilled
engineers that work here,

00:04:26.971 --> 00:04:30.245
have come because of the
mission that you found itself

00:04:30.245 --> 00:04:34.151
to provide a free world class
education for anyone anywhere,

00:04:34.151 --> 00:04:36.501
and folks come here
who are dedicated to it

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:40.911
and who do really spectacular
work on the product.

00:04:40.911 --> 00:04:45.093
So we are building, like I
said, learning experiences,

00:04:45.093 --> 00:04:47.861
we're building teaching experiences,

00:04:47.861 --> 00:04:52.134
and the engineers are building the site.

00:04:54.361 --> 00:04:56.151
So yeah, there's a lot of us

00:04:56.151 --> 00:04:57.384
who are very good.

00:04:59.681 --> 00:05:01.981
- Just to double underline
or triple underline

00:05:01.981 --> 00:05:04.134
what you're saying, I don't
think people realize that,

00:05:04.134 --> 00:05:07.361
you know, when Khan Academy
had a few thousand people

00:05:07.361 --> 00:05:09.843
using it every month, yeah,
maybe it could operate,

00:05:09.843 --> 00:05:12.557
you know, on a shoestring.

00:05:12.557 --> 00:05:14.791
I would say still
operating on a shoestring

00:05:14.791 --> 00:05:15.791
relative to its scale.

00:05:15.791 --> 00:05:17.441
Obviously, it's the budget
of a large High School

00:05:17.441 --> 00:05:19.861
and reaches over 100
million students every year.

00:05:19.861 --> 00:05:22.891
But to your point, you know, we have to

00:05:22.891 --> 00:05:24.601
keep it up and running 24/7,

00:05:24.601 --> 00:05:26.381
we have to make sure it's performant.

00:05:26.381 --> 00:05:28.661
You know, just the data that
we're storing and once again,

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.691
we only store data in service to students

00:05:30.691 --> 00:05:32.061
to make the learning experience better

00:05:32.061 --> 00:05:33.761
or to understand efficacy.

00:05:33.761 --> 00:05:36.041
That by itself is gargantuan.

00:05:36.041 --> 00:05:39.101
We have, you know, millions
of dollars in server costs

00:05:39.101 --> 00:05:41.036
that we have to keep running
as efficiently as possible.

00:05:41.036 --> 00:05:42.181
And to your point,

00:05:42.181 --> 00:05:44.721
our product is more
nuanced and complicated

00:05:44.721 --> 00:05:45.941
than I think most people realize.

00:05:45.941 --> 00:05:49.161
We have official SAT practice,
we have subjects in grades

00:05:49.161 --> 00:05:51.157
from pre k all the way through college,

00:05:51.157 --> 00:05:54.351
we have multiple modalities,
we have teacher aspects,

00:05:54.351 --> 00:05:56.054
tools to it worth keeping
track of everything.

00:05:56.054 --> 00:05:58.501
We have students things
and so you can imagine

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:01.641
just even keeping track
of and the game mechanics

00:06:01.641 --> 00:06:04.331
and the state for 100 million plus people.

00:06:04.331 --> 00:06:09.257
It's no joke, and we're just
kind of glazing the surface.

00:06:09.257 --> 00:06:11.841
So, before we get into,

00:06:11.841 --> 00:06:13.341
there's actually a lot of good
questions that are coming in

00:06:13.341 --> 00:06:14.581
on Facebook and YouTube.

00:06:14.581 --> 00:06:16.341
Before we get into it, Marta, you know,

00:06:16.341 --> 00:06:17.751
just share a little bit of your journey

00:06:17.751 --> 00:06:20.061
of how you got to Khan Academy, you know,

00:06:20.061 --> 00:06:22.031
and start as far back as you like.

00:06:22.031 --> 00:06:23.901
I think that's what's
unique about homeroom.

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:27.504
I think people are intrigued
in knowing folks paths in life.

00:06:28.761 --> 00:06:31.665
- Okay, well we'll go far back.

00:06:31.665 --> 00:06:34.045
So I grew up in Chicago.

00:06:34.045 --> 00:06:36.321
My parents were immigrants.

00:06:36.321 --> 00:06:40.311
I'm a first generation American and

00:06:40.311 --> 00:06:43.521
I think I've shared in
two truths in the light

00:06:43.521 --> 00:06:46.961
So you know, this cell that
one of the things I always

00:06:46.961 --> 00:06:50.231
kind of use is the fact that I
didn't learn to speak English

00:06:50.231 --> 00:06:52.214
until I went to school.

00:06:53.581 --> 00:06:57.344
And so that was my very early journey.

00:06:59.131 --> 00:07:01.991
Coming to Khan Academy is
something that I've often said

00:07:01.991 --> 00:07:05.351
is sort of coming full
circle because I started off

00:07:05.351 --> 00:07:07.841
loving math and studying math.

00:07:07.841 --> 00:07:12.044
And I have a couple of degrees in math.

00:07:14.682 --> 00:07:19.511
As a female in particular,
my age going through

00:07:19.511 --> 00:07:22.331
and being a Math major and
going to graduate school

00:07:22.331 --> 00:07:23.751
the questions that I always got.

00:07:23.751 --> 00:07:25.151
Well, why aren't you in Math Ed?

00:07:25.151 --> 00:07:28.131
because you know, you should be a teacher.

00:07:28.131 --> 00:07:31.221
And I was very adamant about the fact

00:07:31.221 --> 00:07:33.031
that I didn't wanna be a teacher.

00:07:33.031 --> 00:07:36.763
I wanted to just study math.

00:07:36.763 --> 00:07:40.914
And it wasn't until grad school I was a TA

00:07:40.914 --> 00:07:44.581
that I fell in love with teaching.

00:07:44.581 --> 00:07:46.681
And I think a big insight for me there

00:07:46.681 --> 00:07:48.872
which I know you experience
all the time Sal,

00:07:48.872 --> 00:07:50.771
given the content that you do

00:07:50.771 --> 00:07:53.411
is just because I was learning so much

00:07:53.411 --> 00:07:56.291
every time I have to just
really had to talk about

00:07:56.291 --> 00:07:58.781
the same concept even in
classes and they'd repeat

00:07:58.781 --> 00:08:01.141
obviously for grad students as a TA.

00:08:01.141 --> 00:08:02.461
I've learned something new, right?

00:08:02.461 --> 00:08:03.891
There's something else I'd discovered

00:08:03.891 --> 00:08:06.391
some physical phenomenon
that I could relate it to.

00:08:06.391 --> 00:08:08.861
So anyway, I did that for a while,

00:08:08.861 --> 00:08:10.511
I was always in applied mathematics

00:08:10.511 --> 00:08:12.831
and I got really interested in coding

00:08:12.831 --> 00:08:15.531
because I had to do coding to do my work.

00:08:15.531 --> 00:08:18.643
And I eventually studied
computer science as well

00:08:18.643 --> 00:08:22.781
and came out to Silicon Valley for a bit

00:08:22.781 --> 00:08:25.531
to try it out with HP

00:08:25.531 --> 00:08:30.141
and I ended up spending a long
time almost 15 years at HP

00:08:30.141 --> 00:08:32.331
and I have worked in the software business

00:08:32.331 --> 00:08:34.575
I came originally doing CAD algorithms,

00:08:34.575 --> 00:08:37.823
still leveraging my math background a lot

00:08:37.823 --> 00:08:40.381
and then moved to distributed systems

00:08:40.381 --> 00:08:43.721
and really worked long term and
distributed systems research

00:08:43.721 --> 00:08:47.971
at HP Labs and working on
some of the first products

00:08:47.971 --> 00:08:52.548
for broadband distributed
systems on the internet.

00:08:53.761 --> 00:08:56.041
Obviously, the advent of the Internet

00:08:58.201 --> 00:09:00.921
It's something that I experienced.

00:09:00.921 --> 00:09:04.031
And anyway, coming to Khan Academy

00:09:04.031 --> 00:09:06.231
Finally, in my software
development journey

00:09:06.231 --> 00:09:07.968
and leading engineering teams,

00:09:07.968 --> 00:09:10.801
has been kind of like a dream come true

00:09:10.801 --> 00:09:12.181
because it's sort of full circle.

00:09:12.181 --> 00:09:16.490
I was a big fan of Khan
Academy for a long time.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.491
And the fact that I could join and help

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:21.301
with the engineering experience
leadership that I've had

00:09:21.301 --> 00:09:23.099
and contribute to the mission that way

00:09:23.099 --> 00:09:26.001
is pretty special to me.

00:09:26.001 --> 00:09:27.971
- And at least a question from YouTube,

00:09:27.971 --> 00:09:31.751
AZ asks, "Why did you choose
to work with Khan Academy?"

00:09:31.751 --> 00:09:33.141
And I'll add to that question,

00:09:33.141 --> 00:09:34.681
because one of the questions I got

00:09:34.681 --> 00:09:36.891
in the early days of Khan Academy
when I wanted to set it up

00:09:36.891 --> 00:09:38.841
and I did set it up as a not for profit,

00:09:38.841 --> 00:09:40.241
is I got a lot of skepticism

00:09:40.241 --> 00:09:43.151
that you will not be able to
attract top technical talent,

00:09:43.151 --> 00:09:45.050
especially in the Bay Area.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.331
We're literally a few miles down the road

00:09:47.331 --> 00:09:50.051
from Google from Facebook from Apple.

00:09:50.051 --> 00:09:52.701
You know, we pay well,
but we can't give stock

00:09:52.701 --> 00:09:53.900
because we don't have any.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:56.581
Anyone watching has as much
stock as you and I have

00:09:56.581 --> 00:09:57.761
In Khan Academy.

00:09:57.761 --> 00:10:01.379
So you know, you are someone that could be

00:10:01.379 --> 00:10:04.601
the CTO at nearly any
company in the valley.

00:10:04.601 --> 00:10:07.701
And, you know, and I don't
wanna make you tempted,

00:10:07.701 --> 00:10:10.451
but you could be making
millions of dollars every year.

00:10:11.361 --> 00:10:12.194
Why?

00:10:12.194 --> 00:10:14.131
I'll just, you know, that's
just adding to the question

00:10:14.131 --> 00:10:15.181
from AZ on YouTube.

00:10:15.181 --> 00:10:16.731
Why Khan Academy?

00:10:16.731 --> 00:10:17.564
- Why Khan Academy?

00:10:17.564 --> 00:10:19.180
It's a great question.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.901
Well, first of all, I
was a Khan Academy fan

00:10:21.901 --> 00:10:23.421
for a long time.

00:10:23.421 --> 00:10:26.161
And Sal, I know when I
think interviewed with you,

00:10:26.161 --> 00:10:27.789
I shared my stories about like,

00:10:27.789 --> 00:10:30.681
Boy, when I discovered Sal's style site,

00:10:30.681 --> 00:10:33.081
it was basically people would
always ask me to explain,

00:10:33.081 --> 00:10:34.331
like math things to them.

00:10:34.331 --> 00:10:37.241
And I said, you know,
there's this guy (laughs)

00:10:37.241 --> 00:10:40.221
that does so much better
job than I ever did.

00:10:40.221 --> 00:10:42.541
You really should just
kind of look at these.

00:10:42.541 --> 00:10:44.168
And so anyway, I was a big fan.

00:10:44.168 --> 00:10:46.801
And so I feel very privileged

00:10:46.801 --> 00:10:48.351
to come to contribute to the mission.

00:10:48.351 --> 00:10:51.371
Now, I had some trepidation

00:10:51.371 --> 00:10:52.991
about building an engineering team,

00:10:52.991 --> 00:10:55.381
especially when I came
we've scaled very quickly

00:10:55.381 --> 00:10:57.331
in the three years that I've been here.

00:10:57.331 --> 00:11:00.561
The team was less than half
the size that it is today.

00:11:00.561 --> 00:11:04.141
And our growth has been major, I mean,

00:11:04.141 --> 00:11:09.141
just this spring, because
of the COVID-19 situation,

00:11:09.461 --> 00:11:11.751
of course in schools
closing our usage grew,

00:11:11.751 --> 00:11:13.831
as you've talked about quite a bit

00:11:13.831 --> 00:11:15.561
250% very quickly.

00:11:15.561 --> 00:11:20.041
Which, as an aside is
something I'm very, very proud

00:11:20.041 --> 00:11:23.011
of our team about the fact
that we were able to sustain

00:11:23.011 --> 00:11:25.731
that kind of growth and not
cause hiccups for our learners

00:11:25.731 --> 00:11:27.291
when they desperately needed us.

00:11:27.291 --> 00:11:29.601
And when we had all of
those amazing schedules

00:11:29.601 --> 00:11:33.811
and materials, to serve up
for families and students

00:11:33.811 --> 00:11:36.881
who were just all of a sudden at home,

00:11:36.881 --> 00:11:39.004
and everybody was scrambling to adjust.

00:11:41.081 --> 00:11:43.401
Anyway, I did have some trepidation

00:11:43.401 --> 00:11:46.751
about hiring at a nonprofit.

00:11:46.751 --> 00:11:49.501
And I can say that this
is the strongest team

00:11:49.501 --> 00:11:51.747
that I've ever had anywhere.

00:11:51.747 --> 00:11:53.587
Not making this Up.

00:11:53.587 --> 00:11:55.811
This is absolutely the truth.

00:11:55.811 --> 00:11:59.041
We have amazing folks here
who came for the mission

00:11:59.041 --> 00:12:01.151
who come for the mission every day,

00:12:01.151 --> 00:12:04.661
and are also spectacularly
talented and focused

00:12:04.661 --> 00:12:08.559
on this mission and the lever that we have

00:12:08.559 --> 00:12:11.471
in today's world with all of the events

00:12:11.471 --> 00:12:13.047
that are going on today,

00:12:13.047 --> 00:12:16.381
to be able to work somewhere right now.

00:12:16.381 --> 00:12:19.591
And we're all working
very hard, long hours,

00:12:19.591 --> 00:12:21.311
we can talk about some of our projects.

00:12:21.311 --> 00:12:23.831
I don't know that I could do it

00:12:23.831 --> 00:12:26.471
if I wasn't working on
this mission, to be honest,

00:12:26.471 --> 00:12:29.511
