like yesterday as fast as possible.

00:24:47.601 --> 00:24:49.791
And then blue is someone who
likes to think, you know,

00:24:49.791 --> 00:24:51.751
very big picture, maybe out of the box

00:24:51.751 --> 00:24:53.471
and think about what
could the world be like

00:24:53.471 --> 00:24:55.061
and dream about it a little bit

00:24:55.061 --> 00:24:59.151
and in a lot of ways those are
somewhat stereotypical traits

00:24:59.151 --> 00:25:01.141
for people who are entrepreneurial.

00:25:01.141 --> 00:25:03.151
You know, they think of these big ideas

00:25:03.151 --> 00:25:04.171
and they say, okay,

00:25:04.171 --> 00:25:06.691
and I need to try to
build it or prototype it

00:25:06.691 --> 00:25:08.221
or work on it tomorrow.

00:25:08.221 --> 00:25:11.031
And that was great in the
early days of Khan Academy,

00:25:11.031 --> 00:25:12.271
you know, the first four or five years,

00:25:12.271 --> 00:25:15.021
it was literally just me
in this walk in closet,

00:25:15.021 --> 00:25:17.821
having big, big ideas,
but then also saying,

00:25:17.821 --> 00:25:19.461
Let me start hacking this thing together,

00:25:19.461 --> 00:25:20.591
let me code it together.

00:25:20.591 --> 00:25:22.444
And I didn't have a
background in computer science

00:25:22.444 --> 00:25:25.851
but as you grow an organization, you know,

00:25:25.851 --> 00:25:27.371
one of the things to really appreciate

00:25:27.371 --> 00:25:28.661
is where are your strengths?

00:25:28.661 --> 00:25:29.901
And where do you get energy?

00:25:29.901 --> 00:25:31.501
And what are your weaknesses?

00:25:31.501 --> 00:25:32.681
And where do you not get energy

00:25:32.681 --> 00:25:33.631
and there could be weaknesses,

00:25:33.631 --> 00:25:34.651
that you just don't have the skills,

00:25:34.651 --> 00:25:35.771
you don't have the experience.

00:25:35.771 --> 00:25:38.971
And so as Khan Academy has grown,

00:25:38.971 --> 00:25:41.543
I have gained ever increasing appreciation

00:25:41.543 --> 00:25:45.601
for bringing in people who have
the depth and the experience

00:25:45.601 --> 00:25:48.461
to know how to lead teams, scale teams,

00:25:48.461 --> 00:25:50.421
have technical depth that I didn't have.

00:25:50.421 --> 00:25:53.601
And Marta, I think
exemplifies that and you know,

00:25:53.601 --> 00:25:55.881
great people bring in other great people.

00:25:55.881 --> 00:25:59.081
So Three, four years ago, Jenny Lee joined

00:25:59.081 --> 00:26:01.011
as the president and CEO of Khan Academy.

00:26:01.011 --> 00:26:03.674
We'll get Jenny on this,
I think she's being shy.

00:26:03.674 --> 00:26:06.621
We'll get Jenny on the live stream soon.

00:26:06.621 --> 00:26:09.081
But Jenny, you know, just
as Marta could be a leader

00:26:09.081 --> 00:26:12.101
at pretty much any organization
in Silicon Valley or beyond

00:26:12.101 --> 00:26:13.591
Jenny as well could.

00:26:13.591 --> 00:26:17.831
And then Jenny has helped kind
of provide that consistency,

00:26:17.831 --> 00:26:20.321
that process, bringing people along.

00:26:20.321 --> 00:26:22.411
If you have 200 folks,
you can't just you know,

00:26:22.411 --> 00:26:23.521
my instinct is kind of like,

00:26:23.521 --> 00:26:25.241
oh, let's get this done by
this afternoon that done

00:26:25.241 --> 00:26:26.731
but if 200 folks they'll just get

00:26:26.731 --> 00:26:29.222
we'll be moving in different directions.

00:26:29.222 --> 00:26:31.111
So Jenny's provide that leadership

00:26:31.111 --> 00:26:32.451
and one of the best
things about leadership

00:26:32.451 --> 00:26:34.178
is to bring other really amazing people.

00:26:34.178 --> 00:26:35.932
And so Marta was one of the first people

00:26:35.932 --> 00:26:38.431
that Jenny brought in and
Jenny knows how to hire

00:26:38.431 --> 00:26:41.531
then Marta knows how to hire
and develop and build teams.

00:26:41.531 --> 00:26:44.441
We already had a really
good team and it was small

00:26:44.441 --> 00:26:47.191
and Marta's been able to
get them to the next level,

00:26:47.191 --> 00:26:50.290
but then also put in the processes

00:26:50.290 --> 00:26:52.101
and the institutional knowledge

00:26:52.101 --> 00:26:53.841
so that people can really make a career

00:26:53.841 --> 00:26:55.001
out of being at Khan Academy.

00:26:55.001 --> 00:26:57.122
So you know, to answer the
question, what Marta brings,

00:26:57.122 --> 00:27:00.213
you know, these projects,
if Marta wasn't here,

00:27:00.213 --> 00:27:02.601
and if she wasn't able to lead
the team that she's leading

00:27:02.601 --> 00:27:04.880
in the way that she's doing it,

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:06.780
I don't think it's an exaggeration
to say when COVID hit,

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:09.271
a lot of y'all might not have been able

00:27:09.271 --> 00:27:10.851
to access Khan Academy (laughs)

00:27:10.851 --> 00:27:12.951
I mean, that's just in plain, you know,

00:27:12.951 --> 00:27:14.891
and there would have
been millions of people

00:27:14.891 --> 00:27:17.021
who would have not been able to learn.

00:27:17.021 --> 00:27:19.601
So, because our site probably wouldn't

00:27:19.601 --> 00:27:22.481
have been able to scale
or even if it was working,

00:27:22.481 --> 00:27:24.281
it would probably take
in 20 seconds for you

00:27:24.281 --> 00:27:25.741
to access certain pages,

00:27:25.741 --> 00:27:26.891
which would have been very frustrating

00:27:26.891 --> 00:27:29.011
for millions of millions of people.

00:27:29.011 --> 00:27:30.851
So I think that's one
level and the other thing

00:27:30.851 --> 00:27:32.761
which Marta does is, you know,

00:27:32.761 --> 00:27:35.421
I have so much confidence
in Marta and her team,

00:27:35.421 --> 00:27:39.501
that it allows me to focus more energy

00:27:39.501 --> 00:27:43.528
on where I think I might
have value, you know, of,

00:27:43.528 --> 00:27:45.801
what's the future of
Khan Academy going to be

00:27:45.801 --> 00:27:47.501
where we're going to be
in five years, you know,

00:27:47.501 --> 00:27:49.371
I've spent a lot of time fundraising,

00:27:49.371 --> 00:27:50.369
I still make a lot of content.

00:27:50.369 --> 00:27:52.099
And it's not that I'm detached.

00:27:52.099 --> 00:27:54.888
I'm deeply interested and
I want to support Marta

00:27:54.888 --> 00:27:56.661
and the team wherever I can

00:27:56.661 --> 00:27:58.528
but when you have really amazing leaders,

00:27:58.528 --> 00:28:00.881
leaders that you can learn from, you know,

00:28:00.881 --> 00:28:02.837
I learned from Marta, I learned from Jenny

00:28:02.837 --> 00:28:06.141
and many others every
day about their craft,

00:28:06.141 --> 00:28:09.371
about how to lead people,
about how to lead an org.

00:28:09.371 --> 00:28:12.151
That just takes a lot off of my plate.

00:28:12.151 --> 00:28:14.881
So yeah, I consider myself
very, very fortunate

00:28:14.881 --> 00:28:17.351
to have Marta on this team.

00:28:17.351 --> 00:28:21.161
- Well, that's very,
very nice of you to say,

00:28:21.161 --> 00:28:23.694
and we're very fortunate to be here.

00:28:25.311 --> 00:28:27.341
- Oh, thank you. So this is from,

00:28:27.341 --> 00:28:29.791
oh, time always runs out
fast when we're having fun,

00:28:29.791 --> 00:28:31.991
but we I think we have
time for maybe a few more.

00:28:31.991 --> 00:28:34.061
And this is a good question.

00:28:34.061 --> 00:28:36.131
And maybe we could both
take a shot at this one

00:28:36.131 --> 00:28:40.751
from YouTube, Nico Raj asks,
"How do you face criticism

00:28:40.751 --> 00:28:42.838
after having failed at something?"

00:28:44.241 --> 00:28:47.441
Marta have you ever failed in anything?

00:28:47.441 --> 00:28:50.854
- I have failed in something
every day. (laughs)

00:28:54.061 --> 00:28:55.161
- So how did you deal with it?

00:28:55.161 --> 00:28:56.741
like, tell me something that you failed,

00:28:56.741 --> 00:28:57.738
that kind of hit you.

00:28:57.738 --> 00:28:59.641
And maybe even you got criticism.

00:28:59.641 --> 00:29:01.034
How did you cope?

00:29:02.681 --> 00:29:03.791
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:29:03.791 --> 00:29:06.091
I'll think of one and you
think of one at the same time.

00:29:06.091 --> 00:29:08.011
See, if we don't have much time left,

00:29:08.011 --> 00:29:09.421
what would be a good one to talk about

00:29:09.421 --> 00:29:12.551
I certainly have an abundance
of choices of mistakes

00:29:12.551 --> 00:29:13.384
that I make.

00:29:13.384 --> 00:29:15.711
I mean, I'm serious when
I say that I make mistakes

00:29:15.711 --> 00:29:17.621
all the time.

00:29:17.621 --> 00:29:22.621
And I learned from my team all
the time, literally every day

00:29:23.121 --> 00:29:27.050
and gain insights from
what folks that are doing.

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:31.741
And let me think of a spectacular failure,

00:29:31.741 --> 00:29:34.714
like Yeah, because that's
probably a better story.

00:29:38.491 --> 00:29:41.291
- We can compete for largest failures.

00:29:41.291 --> 00:29:42.124
- Yeah.

00:29:42.124 --> 00:29:43.861
Do you have one you're ready to share?

00:29:43.861 --> 00:29:45.291
I'm still--

00:29:45.291 --> 00:29:46.124
- Well, I have many.

00:29:46.124 --> 00:29:49.801
I mean, from an economic point
of view, people don't know.

00:29:49.801 --> 00:29:51.341
So I was working at a hedge fund

00:29:51.341 --> 00:29:52.821
and then my boss wanted to retire a

00:29:52.821 --> 00:29:56.441
nd he wanted me to essentially
start my own hedge fund.

00:29:56.441 --> 00:29:58.261
This was pre Khan Academy.

00:29:58.261 --> 00:30:00.741
And I essentially took
over his cost structure,

00:30:00.741 --> 00:30:04.891
and I lost and this is, you
know, I didn't own a house,

00:30:04.891 --> 00:30:06.641
the only money me and my family had

00:30:06.641 --> 00:30:09.401
was essentially a down payment
we were saving up for a house

00:30:09.401 --> 00:30:12.551
and I lost almost $200,000.

00:30:12.551 --> 00:30:16.051
And so you can imagine our
down payment for a house

00:30:16.051 --> 00:30:18.741
quickly became not
sufficient to buy a house.

00:30:18.741 --> 00:30:22.669
And so I took long a lot
walks in the hills out here

00:30:22.669 --> 00:30:23.502
in Silicon Valley,

00:30:25.102 --> 00:30:27.641
trying to save my ego.

00:30:27.641 --> 00:30:29.911
And, you know, I would look
at squirrels and I said,

00:30:29.911 --> 00:30:32.461
we know that squirrel doesn't
know I just lost $200,000

00:30:32.461 --> 00:30:33.951
maybe we can be friends.

00:30:33.951 --> 00:30:37.591
And that kind of (laughs)
gave me because it affects you

00:30:37.591 --> 00:30:38.571
in very deep ways.

00:30:38.571 --> 00:30:40.291
I think we're oftentimes
our biggest critics

00:30:40.291 --> 00:30:41.571
and we start saying, you know,

00:30:41.571 --> 00:30:42.911
what will my family think of me?

00:30:42.911 --> 00:30:45.331
What will my friends think of me?

00:30:45.331 --> 00:30:47.191
I've been such a massive failure.

00:30:47.191 --> 00:30:49.151
Will I ever be able to recover from this?

00:30:49.151 --> 00:30:50.931
Will people ever want to work with me

00:30:50.931 --> 00:30:52.021
when they've seen this failure?

00:30:52.021 --> 00:30:53.241
Will they hire me?

00:30:53.241 --> 00:30:55.455
You know, I mean, there's
really big questions.

00:30:55.455 --> 00:30:58.101
You know, in terms of
how do you cope with it,

00:30:58.101 --> 00:30:59.751
you realize when you
go through these things

00:30:59.751 --> 00:31:04.084
over and over again in
your life, that they pass,

00:31:04.084 --> 00:31:06.241
and things do get better,

00:31:06.241 --> 00:31:08.291
as long as you put one
foot in front of the other.

00:31:08.291 --> 00:31:10.051
And so now that I've had, you know,

00:31:10.051 --> 00:31:11.241
I've had probably four or five

00:31:11.241 --> 00:31:12.711
of these big failures in my life,

00:31:12.711 --> 00:31:14.461
and I've had probably four or 500

00:31:14.461 --> 00:31:16.196
of lesser failures in my life,

00:31:16.196 --> 00:31:19.442
that in that day also
seemed fairly catastrophic.

00:31:19.442 --> 00:31:21.901
I realized that as long as I put one foot

00:31:21.901 --> 00:31:23.291
in front of the other,
have a positive attitude,

00:31:23.291 --> 00:31:24.591
keep meditating,

00:31:24.591 --> 00:31:27.141
a month later, I don't
even remember what that was

00:31:27.141 --> 00:31:29.241
or a year later, I can
kind of talk about it

00:31:29.241 --> 00:31:31.741
on a live stream like I'm
a you know, a wise soul.

00:31:33.684 --> 00:31:38.121
- Yeah, to get back to
our meditation, kind of,

00:31:38.121 --> 00:31:40.921
you know, a Buddhist philosophy roots

00:31:40.921 --> 00:31:43.031
and mean a big part of
it is just, you know,

00:31:43.031 --> 00:31:44.048
the stories we tell ourselves

00:31:44.048 --> 00:31:47.391
and so, not believing yourself.

00:31:47.391 --> 00:31:48.224
It's true.

00:31:48.224 --> 00:31:51.181
I mean, the most important
thing is, we are ourselves.

00:31:51.181 --> 00:31:53.541
We're all born as this
pretty perfect little beings

00:31:53.541 --> 00:31:55.881
and we do get screwed up along the way

00:31:55.881 --> 00:31:58.840
With various things but we are, you know,

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:01.061
that's still there, right?

00:32:01.061 --> 00:32:03.431
We all have, you know,
the bad and the good

00:32:03.431 --> 00:32:06.878
and we all can get better every day

00:32:06.878 --> 00:32:08.841
and so not calling yourself a screw up

00:32:08.841 --> 00:32:11.211
or thinking that you screwed up so badly

00:32:11.211 --> 00:32:12.731
that no one should love you is

00:32:12.731 --> 00:32:17.701
really important way to
leave lead your life, I think

00:32:17.701 --> 00:32:18.751
- That's exactly right.

00:32:18.751 --> 00:32:21.921
I mean, of the Buddhist or
Eastern or Vedic philosophy,

00:32:21.921 --> 00:32:25.021
there's all this notion of non attachment,

00:32:25.021 --> 00:32:27.751
that it's only when we get
attached to our personas

00:32:27.751 --> 00:32:29.551
or these narratives around our personas

00:32:29.551 --> 00:32:31.471
or attached to materiality,

00:32:31.471 --> 00:32:33.701
that that's actually the cause
of suffering, true suffering,

00:32:33.701 --> 00:32:35.141
you know, there's pain
which is you're getting,

00:32:35.141 --> 00:32:36.711
you know, there's a needle in your arm,

00:32:36.711 --> 00:32:38.661
there's suffering, which
is the mental state

00:32:38.661 --> 00:32:40.981
where you know, you kind
of catastrophize things

00:32:40.981 --> 00:32:42.591
and it causes anxiety

00:32:42.591 --> 00:32:44.991
but when you realize that, you
know, there's a part of you

00:32:44.991 --> 00:32:47.151
that's pure than that,
that is not your persona,

00:32:47.151 --> 00:32:49.161
that is not your identity,
that is not your role,

00:32:49.161 --> 00:32:52.611
that is not your name, but it's
actually very peace inducing

00:32:52.611 --> 00:32:53.801
and liberating and actually helps you

00:32:53.801 --> 00:32:56.691
get out of your rut many times.

00:32:56.691 --> 00:32:58.534
So not to preach too much.

00:33:00.031 --> 00:33:01.044
But Marta, thanks so much.

00:33:01.044 --> 00:33:02.291
I mean, there's so many more questions.

00:33:02.291 --> 00:33:03.871
I think we will do this again,

00:33:03.871 --> 00:33:07.066
if this wasn't a horrible event for you.

00:33:07.066 --> 00:33:09.851
(laughs)

00:33:09.851 --> 00:33:12.341
But thank you so much for
joining, we will do this again.

00:33:12.341 --> 00:33:15.821
And thanks everyone for asking questions.

00:33:15.821 --> 00:33:18.061
So, once again, you
know, this is always fun.

00:33:18.061 --> 00:33:20.144
We have these great conversations,
I encourage everyone

00:33:20.144 --> 00:33:23.431
to join us tomorrow for
ongoing conversations,

00:33:23.431 --> 00:33:26.241
and we're gonna be talking
about kind of child development

00:33:26.241 --> 00:33:28.106
and how we raise our children
and generational changes.

00:33:28.106 --> 00:33:31.004
So thanks, everyone for
joining today's livestream.

